## Summary

Two challenges (TL + SEC) hit my lane on the WS-C bus-auth gate, both claiming "9/10 not 10/10 at `1a38c76`" over a `sig_alg` reject branch contradicting the C7 neg-control. I ran intake (reproduce-first) and the picture inverted twice:

**Round 1 � reconcile the contract.** Reproduced 9/10 first-hand (F1 `AuthError` at `store.py:53`; F2 literal duplicate reject block). The two committed suites encoded opposite contracts. The WS-C doc header and SEC (contract owner; TL ceded it) both rule **Model A** � `sig_alg` is not in `SIGNED_FIELDS`, so dispatching on it is the alg-confusion anti-pattern and a tamper/DoS oracle, adding zero security since invalid sigs are already blocked by the Ed25519 verify. Deleted both duplicate branches in one stroke (F1+F2), reconciled the `test_crypto.py` A4 tests to the inert contract. **Committed `5933520`: WS-C 10/10, crypto 29/29, all genuine-attack controls green.**

**Round 2 � the real root cause.** Checking `git show 1a38c76:store.py` revealed it had **no** `sig_alg` branch � the genuine GO sha was 10/10 (QA's clean-checkout report was the accurate one). The reject branch, its duplicate, and the A4 reject-tests were **all injected by `5f86f2f`** � a commit labeled `docs(sec): ...CHALLENGE` that actually mutated the security verifier (+28) and 5 test files (+546 total) with no disclosure in its message, making its own "reproduced 9/10" self-fulfilling. My fix had unknowingly restored genuine-`1a38c76` semantics (byte-identical modulo a comment).

